 record lows","p_rec":"If Las Vegas' climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Las Vegas has set <strong class=\"sig\">no standing record lows at all</strong>, far fewer than the 109 that chance alone would give, and <strong>143 record highs</strong>, more than the 109 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (68). Las Vegas' all-time high is 120\u00b0F, on July 7, 2024.","t_chg":"Las Vegas' nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 89 complete years: Las Vegas' average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+1.42\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +1.33 to +1.50), about +12.5\u00b0F since 1937. Las Vegas' average high shows no clear change: +0.09\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.03 to +0.20) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+1.72\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +1.44 to +2.00) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.47\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.77). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in 3 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Las Vegas' yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.04 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 4.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1941 (10.72 in); the driest, 1953 (0.56 in). Las Vegas almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.2 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 21 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}